-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       The New Zealand Story
  Publisher   Ocean, 1989
  Version     1.1

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch
  (one of these sets, every file of the set together):

  Set 1
  File        New Zealand Story, The (1989)(Ocean)(48K-128K).tap
  MD5         d5f47d90cbfbed45a4da7bf86637dfd3
  File        New Zealand Story, The (1989)(Ocean)(48K-128K)(Side B).tzx
  MD5         f1c1ee16beacf5c9b4ef19c5b539765e

  Set 2
  File        New Zealand Story, The (1989)(Ocean)(48K-128K).tzx
  MD5         b0711e0daa7e1906e641ecfe513a0c7e
  File        New Zealand Story, The (1989)(Ocean)(48K-128K)(Side B).tzx
  MD5         f1c1ee16beacf5c9b4ef19c5b539765e

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Pace        slowdown removed (-10.19% measured in JNext 1.1.104, 10 runs per
              scene)

  Changes     1.0: the colour build (tested by Dean Woodyatt). 1.1: busy
              scenes no longer slow down; sprite/scenery handover and
              occasional scroll jumps fixed; Smooth scrolling reworked so the
              camera moves evenly.

  Note        The whole game in colour: the scenery is drawn on the Next's
              tilemap from the game's own map, and every figure, fruit and
              item is a painted sprite. It loads from Ocean's two tape files,
              the Side A .tap (or .tzx) and the Side B .tzx, kept together in
              the patch's folder. Starts at the title screen. The busy scenes
              no longer slow down: the game's own drawing runs at 28 MHz,
              capped at the original's quiet-scene pace, with its logic and
              sound at 3.5 MHz. Smooth scrolling, off by default, glides the
              camera between the game's steps. Needs a core with the tilemap
              (any recent one).

-- MENU ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   1  Infinite lives
   2  No enemies
   3  Immunity to pain!
   4  Unlimited oxygen

